A||||10||deceler8||Pickup type sliding rear window...||||5||
Z||000000||deceler8||01-14-2000||12:30 PM||||My car has no sunroof...and I'm wondering why no one ever came up with a sliding rear window for these cars. <P>Once the car is moving, I can't imagine the heat from the vents being a problem, and how much worse could the noise be...<P>On the surface, it doesn't seem like a bad idea, but what do I know...||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ditz88GT||01-14-2000||01:10 PM||||I always thought it would be cool to do something like the back window on the Honda Del Sols.||||reg||6
Z||000002||ChadMan||01-14-2000||01:11 PM||||Y'know, I toyed with the idea of replacing the glass rear window with a removable plexiglass one (like the 68-73 Corvette).  But I was a little afraid of fumes and heat coming into the cockpit.  That, and I'm lazy.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||01-14-2000||01:30 PM||||I found a website where a guy did the DelSol "conversion." He said the noise and heat wasn't really a problem until he changed the seats in the car. Then he felt a lot more heat hitting the back of his neck. If you don't think much heat is coming out of those vents, drive down the highway for several miles, then put your hand on the inside of the glass. It is quite warm, even warmer just in font of the vents. I've always been intrigued with the idea of making a powered roll down rear window. But I always wonder if it will affect the low pressure behind the glass that sucks the air through the vents.||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||01-14-2000||02:09 PM||||Alright, I'm not up on my foreign cars...just exactly what kind of rear window does the Del Sol have ?<P>And I was thinking of something that opened in the middle for ventilation, not a removing type rear window, though that would be cool.<P>A sunroof-type flip out on the rear window would be cool too...wouldn't mess up the vacuum that pulls heat out of the vents.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Scott2M4||01-14-2000||02:35 PM||||Honda's two-seater had an electric rear window - similar in design to the window in the tailgate of those older full-size American station wagons (or Toyota's 4-Runner).  The entire window rolls down behind the seats.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||01-14-2000||02:47 PM||||Oh, you mean like early-mid '60's full size Mercurys ? I always thought those cars were massive and ugly, but was always intrigued by the electric rear window.<P>Rolling a window behind the seat would be a neat trick on the Fieros. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||01-14-2000||02:55 PM||||If I remember correctly.. V8 Archie's Chop Top has a Del Sol-type window in the back. He mentioned offering it as a conversion, along with the hood scoops (not decklid), but it's not on his page anywhere, and the original Chop Top page is gone. (the one detailing the construction)||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||01-14-2000||04:25 PM||||personal opinoion... if you were able to put in a sliding or drop down window, it would A) screw up the vacum effect and b) draw into the cockpit a considerable amount of fumes when the car is stoped or going slowly.  not to mention the heat.||||reg||2
Z||000009||lowCG||01-14-2000||04:46 PM||||Mercury Monterey's are pretty cool.<BR>Maybe you could get a window from an old pickup,and cut it down to fit into your car?<BR>Or do it with Lexan,using the hardware from the truck window.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dave85||01-14-2000||08:34 PM||||When I had my exhaust leak I would get a good whiff when the sunroof was open.  If you've ever seen how smoke or water comes up through the vents, it really rolls behind the glass.||||reg||1
